M
elf elves~
He shrugs, pauses a bit to think about his answer and says, "I get along with
the elves.  They appreciate my work, and their mages come in quite handy for
certain projects.  They go out of their way to try to keep me supplied with
the rare metals I like to work with."  He shrugs and chuckles.  "Ok, the truth
is my god came to me in a dream and told me to come here.  So, I am here.  I
am very happy here really, these elves have had to fight hard to survive, they
don't make friends quickly, but when they do call you friend you can trust them
with your soul."
~
M
metal metals mithril~
"Ahh, now there is a subject true to my heart.  Most good smiths will tell you
that mithril is the finest metal to work with.  It is, mostly.  Adamantium
makes slightly tougher armor but is more difficult to work with and doesn't
keep an edge as well.  Blue steel makes good weapons, and fair armor, but
isn't near as nice to work with as either adamantium or mithril.  Mithril
a bit easier to get than adamantium and is what I generally prefer to work
with.  I mix a bit of blue steel in with it, it seems to strengthen it a bit
for some strange reason, believe me, I have done many tests.  The odd thing
is that the mixture makes a green-hued metal, but I find the color appealing
and the elves love it.  The most challenging things to work with are the
scales or tough hides of dragons and the like.  They are more difficult to
work with but make excellent armor, especially if you strengthen the joints
with mithril."  He suddenly notices that you have become lost in his long
discourse and says, "Oh, sorry, bit more information than you wanted eh?
Talk of metals just gets me going."
~
