learned one banitoor vorsileez~
   Her musical voice seems to sing her reply to you, '&+cBanitoor Vorsileez is
&+chighly respected amongst my people and the people of my cousin, Hateeu.  She
&+cwas once a drow priestess in the service of the dread god Dryzkul, but she
&+cabandoned the ways of her people.  She wandered into our glen one dark evening
&+cmany centuries ago and was captured by my grandfather's scouts.  I was but a
&+cyoung philly then, fresh out of the womb.  My grandfather sentenced her to
&+cdeath by torture, but first he wished to interrogate her about why she was
&+chere.&n'

   Tamilea shifts her weight from one hoof to another, then continues, '&+cFor
&+cthree nights he interrogated her.  On the morning following their last
&+cinterview, my grandfather announced he needed more time to get infomation from
&+cher.  This continued for three weeks, by the end of which my grandfather had
&+cfallen in love with her.  She warned him of a drow raid that she had been sent
&+cto spearhead.  She also told him how to best defend our people against her
&+cbrothers and sisters.  When my grandfather announce that Banitoor would not be
&+cexecuted, his people turned against him.  They said she had poisoned his mind,
&+cand said his lust was what drove him to such madness.&n'

   She chokes back a sob, '&+cThey set about to execute both my grandfather and
&+cthe priestess, and had succeeded in grandfather's punishment.  Before they
&+ccould execute the drow woman, her evil brethren attacked.  My people were
&+cfrantic, many of them died before the hands of the dread elves before Banitoor
&+cjoined in the defense of our forest.  She hurled a spell into the midst of the
&+cbattle and took out many of the evil warriors.  She roused my people out of a
&+cfast growing despair and led them to victory.  They not only let her live when
&+ceverything was over, but they gave her the highest honor in out culture.  An
&+chonor that had not been borne in many years.&n'

   '&+cI tell you this for three reasons.  The first being the obvious, she is a
&+cdrow.  I would not send you to meet her without telling you this, so you can
&+cleave your prejudice behind.  The second is so that you understand her
&+ccharacter, so you can fathom who she is despite the color of her skin.  And
&+cfinally so that you have no illusions about whom you are going to see, and how
&+cstrongly we will fight to protect her,&n' she looks you deep in the eyes.  '&+cYou
&+ccan find her cave near the southwest corner of my cousin's plains.  Do not
&+cgive up the search if at first u do not find her cave.  Fare thee well, and
&+cpray do not do anything foolish.&n'
~
